  "id": "yiddish_theatre_second_avenue",
  "name": "Yiddish theatre song (Second Avenue operetta)",
  "family": "pop",
  "lineage": "Song and operetta of the Yiddish theatre of New York's Lower East Side and Second Avenue from the 1880s to the 1950s, where composers such as Joseph Rumshinsky, Sholom Secunda, Abraham Ellstein and Alexander Olshanetsky wrote for stars including Boris Thomashefsky, Molly Picon and Aaron Lebedeff. Show tunes and comic patter songs mixed Eastern European Jewish modes and cantorial-style melisma with waltzes, marches, tangos and American popular-song forms, sung over a pit orchestra of strings, clarinet, brass and piano and widely issued on 78 rpm records.",
  "recipe": "classic mid-1880s yiddish theatre song, spoken Word, stage performance branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. violin orchestral, clarinet, yellow brass canonical 70 30 bach stradivarius bach stradivarius 180 canonical American orchestral trumpet, upright piano, double bass. blanket damped 1925-1945 commercial-major electrical era office. acoustic horn. 78rpm shellac.",
